body:
Oh it does notify.

Generally things are just much simpler when you treat closures as boundaries for type inference. That way you can analyse the closure body, verify the function works as expected, and separately verify that the stated closure signature matches the one that the closure's being passed to.

Consider this code:

```php
/**
 * @param Collection<Optional<string>> $collection
 */
function expandOptions(Collection $collection) {
  $a = function (Optional $optional) {
    return $optional->get();
  };
  return $collection->map($a);
}
```

We shouldn't have to re-analyse the closure body `$a` after it's passed to `map` - analysing it once should be sufficient.

Another potential issue here is that Psalm doesn't _insist_ on a signature param type having all its generic params defined (so `Optional` is allowed where it should probably insist on a `Optional<...>` annotation). Feel free to ticket that.

> We shouldn't have to re-analyse the closure body $a after it's passed to map - analysing it once should be sufficient.

You might be right, one could argue either way, but that's a bit different to my case because you're assigning the closure to a variable when declaring it, at which point you can't necessarily infer param and return types, whereas I'm declaring a closure within a context from which the types can be safely inferred. The two cases are treated differently by TypeScript and Java I believe, and I would assume most other implementations of generics.

To clarify:

```php
/**
 * @param Collection<Optional<string>> $collection
 */
function expandOptions(Collection $collection) {
  // we can't infer the param/return types yet -- fair enough
  $a = function (Optional $optional) {
    return $optional->get();
  };
  return $collection->map($a);
}
```

versus

```php
/**
 * @param Collection<Optional<string>> $collection
 */
function expandOptions(Collection $collection) {
  // we can safely infer the param and return types here
  return $collection->map(function ($optional) {
    return $optional->get();
  });
}
```

I can certainly understand the first approach requiring a hint above the assignment, but for the very common case of passing a closure as an argument to another function, which will become even more common with arrow functions, it just adds noise, which is why in most other languages those types are inferred and developers don't specify them.

I'm afraid it's not clear to me whether the lack of inference in this case is your preferred approach regardless of implementation complexity within Psalm itself, or if it's your preference because of the complexity it would add to Psalm to infer param and return types in these cases. If it's the latter then it's probably not worth us discussing any further -- you're the expert, and if it's too hard to do then it's too hard to do!

Here is a **TypeScript** example. You can ignore the transpiled JavaScript.

https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/index.html#src=interface%20Collection%3CE%3E%20%7B%0D%0A%20%20%20%20map%3CR%3E(mapper%3A%20(element%3A%20E)%20%3D%3E%20R)%3A%20Collection%3CR%3E%3B%0D%0A%7D%0D%0A%0D%0Ainterface%20Optional%3CT%3E%20%7B%0D%0A%20%20%20%20get()%3A%20T%3B%0D%0A%7D%0D%0A%0D%0Afunction%20expandOptions1%3CT%3E(optionals%3A%20Collection%3COptional%3CT%3E%3E)%3A%20Collection%3CT%3E%20%7B%0D%0A%20%20%20%20return%20optionals.map(e%20%3D%3E%20e.foo())%3B%0D%0A%7D%0D%0A%0D%0Afunction%20expandOptions2%3CT%3E(optionals%3A%20Collection%3COptional%3CT%3E%3E)%3A%20Collection%3CT%3E%20%7B%0D%0A%20%20%20%20const%20fn%20%3D%20e%20%3D%3E%20e.foo()%3B%0D%0A%20%20%20%20return%20optionals.map(fn)%3B%0D%0A%7D

**Java**

```java
import java.util.stream.Stream;
import java.util.Optional;

class Playground {
    public static void main(String[ ] args) {}
    
    public static <T> Stream<T> expandOptions1(Stream<Optional<T>> stream) {
        return stream.map(optional -> optional.get());
    }
    
    public static <T> Stream<T> expandOptions2(Stream<Optional<T>> stream) {
        return stream.map(optional -> optional.foo());
    }
    
    public static <T> Stream<T> expandOptions3(Stream<Optional<T>> stream) {
        var fn = optional -> optional.get();
        return stream.map(fn);
    }
}
```

Compiler output:

```
..\Playground\:12: error: cannot find symbol
        return stream.map(optional -> optional.foo());
                                              ^
  symbol:   method foo()
  location: variable optional of type Optional<T>
  where T is a type-variable:
    T extends Object declared in method <T>expandOptions2(Stream<Optional<T>>)
..\Playground\:16: error: cannot infer type for local variable fn
        var fn = optional -> optional.get();
            ^
  (lambda expression needs an explicit target-type)
2 errors
```

What you are seeing here is that `expandOptions1` compiles fine, `expandOptions2` fails because `Optional.foo()` is non-existent (Java has inferred that optional is an instance of Optional), and `expandOptions3` fails because the type of `fn` cannot be inferred.

issue.body:
could psalm infer a closure param type if the input to array_map was taken from a typed variadic ? (i.e. to upgrade the error message from "could not infer type")

```php
<?php
function Foo(DateTime ...$dateTimes) : array {
  return array_map(function ($dateTime) : string {
    return (string) ($dateTime->format('c'));
  }, $dateTimes);
}
```
